course: dictionary
lesson: 10024
title: Tendrils: thin, curling climbers
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A tendril is a thin, curly, thread-like part of a climbing plant. It wraps around things, like a stick, to help the plant climb up towards the light. Look at the words around the word to find its meaning.
explain: «Pea tendrils feel touch and curl around whatever they meet, sometimes within hours.» A climbing plant needs something to hold, so a tendril must be a grabbing curl. Try your meaning in the sentence: «Pea tendrils are thin curly threads that wrap around whatever they meet» still makes sense, so you have it.
explain: The word can also describe a thin curl of something that is not a plant. In a story, «tendrils of fear» means fear slowly creeping in, like a thin curl that spreads. Same idea, different picture.
fact: A tendril is a thin, curly, thread-like part of a climbing plant.
fact: Tendrils wrap around sticks and other supports to help plants climb towards the light.
fact: «Pea tendrils feel touch and curl around whatever they meet.»
fact: A tendril can also be a thin curl of something like smoke or hair, such as «a tendril of hair».
fact: Ivy climbs in a different way, with tiny gripping roots, not with tendrils.
exercise: «Pea plants climb with tendrils that wrap round whatever they touch.» What is a tendril? | *A thin, curly, thread-like part that wraps round things | A leaf that stores food | A tiny gripping root | A thick stem that holds the plant up
exercise: «A mermaid's purse has curly tendrils at its four corners.» What do the tendrils do? | They turn the egg into a fish | They break off in the sea | They make the egg case float away | *They hold the egg case to seaweed
exercise: Find and copy the word that means thin, curly threads that wrap round things. | light | stem | *tendrils | ivy
test: «Pea plants climb with tendrils that wrap round whatever they touch, reaching the light without building a thick stem.» What do the tendrils help the pea plant do? | Sink underground | *Climb up towards the light | Store food in swollen leaves | Grow thick like a tree
test: Why do tendrils matter in plant science? | They stop plants from growing | They show a plant is dead | *They let climbing plants reach the light without a thick stem | They make seeds fall off the plant
exam: In a story, «tendrils of fear» means fear slowly creeping in. What does «tendrils of» suggest? | Something sweet and sticky | *Something thin that creeps in slowly | Something loud that bursts out | Something heavy that falls to the ground
